        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2c8f02b5-6c23-5566-be64-9734d00363cc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Edmund Bacon, Thomas Jefferson\n- Places: Monticello, Charlottesville, Virginia, Piedmont region\n- Roles: business manager, primary overseer, President of the United States\n- Topics: plantation agriculture, slavery, tobacco cultivation, wheat cultivation\n\nFacts:\n- Thomas Jefferson began designing and building Monticello at age 26 after inheriting the land from his father.\n- Monticello was the primary plantation of Thomas Jefferson, the third President of the United States.\n- Monticello was located just outside Charlottesville, Virginia, in the Piedmont region.\n- Monticello originally comprised 5,000 acres.\n- Thomas Jefferson used enslaved people at Monticello for extensive cultivation of tobacco and mixed crops.\n- Thomas Jefferson later shifted Monticello’s agriculture from tobacco to wheat in response to changing markets.\n- Edmund Bacon (1785–1866) served Thomas Jefferson at Monticello as his business manager and primary overseer for 20 years.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
